Grader verified — and it's better than I expected:

```js
grade(ws) {
  writeFileSync(join(ws,'hidden_grade_test.py'), HIDDEN_TESTS);
  const out = execSync('python3 -m unittest hidden_grade_test 2>&1', {cwd: ws, timeout: 120000, ...});
  return /^OK\b/m.test(out) && /Ran\s+(\d+)\s+test/.test(out) && +out.match(...)[1] > 0;
}
```

The **held-out suite is written at grade time** (the agent never sees it), it runs stdlib-only with a timeout, and critically it guards `Ran N > 0` — which blocks the classic false pass where zero tests collected still prints `OK`. Failures/timeouts fall through to `false`. That's an honest grader, so the uncapped **PASS** is real: the 27B actually implemented the ledger to spec against unseen data.

That also retires my earlier worry — the task isn't too hard or ungradeable, so the sweep will measure context policy rather than task difficulty.

Now waiting on two background jobs: the **7-cell sweep** (~10 min/cell, logging to `reports/metrics/context-dedup/ab-longbuild-run.log`) and the **adversarial code review**. I'll report the cell table and the review findings together, and write `report-ab-longbuild.md` once the numbers are in.

